insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 1, 'fitCoreyToKrTable minimizes residuals of log10(kr) rather than kr itself. What problem does the logarithm solve?', '["It guarantees convergence in fewer iterations, because a log objective is quadratic in the exponents and needs no line search", "It removes the need for the krFloor by making zero values representable", "It keeps the high-kr end from dominating, so the low-kr decades that carry the exponent count equally", "It converts the bounded problem into an unbounded one the kernel can solve exactly"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In plain kr space a large error at kr near 0.0006 contributes almost nothing next to a small error at kr near 0.3, so the fit would trade away the low decades entirely. Taking log10 first makes a factor-of-two mismatch cost the same wherever it occurs, which is also how the lab actually measures a permeability ratio. Zeros remain unrepresentable in log space, which is the floor''s job, and convergence speed and bounds are unrelated to the choice.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 2, 'On the 13-row Ekene lab grid the fit reports pointsUsed = 24. Where did the other two curve points go?', '["The connate krw of 0 and the residual kro of 0 fall at or below the krFloor and are dropped", "The two rows nearest the front saturation are held out as a validation pair and rejoined after convergence", "Duplicate saturations were merged during the table''s internal sort, collapsing two rows into one anchor", "The optimizer discarded the two points with the largest residuals as outliers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Thirteen rows carry twenty six curve points. The endpoint zeros are definitional, true for every exponent, so they carry no fitting information, and their logarithms do not exist; the krFloor of 1e-4 excludes exactly those two points on this table. The engine has no hold-out set, no outlier rejection, and this table has no duplicate saturations.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 3, 'The fit on the noise-free Ekene grid returns nw = 2.4999999999999996 with rmsLog = 1.3784958753881249e-16. What is the correct reading of these two numbers together?', '["The fit stopped one iteration early and a tighter tolerance would return 2.5 exactly", "Recovery is exact to double precision: both numbers sit at the scale of machine rounding", "The table carries a trace of measurement noise that biased the exponent low by a few parts in 1e16", "The krFloor removed too many points and weakened the estimate to the edge of usability"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An rmsLog of 1.4e-16 says the model and the table agree to the last bit a 64-bit float can hold, so there is no misfit for a tighter tolerance to remove; 2.4999999999999996 is simply where the minimum lands in double precision. The table is synthetic and noise-free, and the floor removed only the two definitional zeros.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 4, 'Which statement about the exponent bounds in fitCoreyToKrTable is correct?', '["The bounds are estimated from the data before the fit begins", "nw is bounded but no is left free, because the oil curve is the better determined of the two", "The bounds only apply when fitEndpoints is true", "Both exponents are bounded to 0.5 through 8, the range the parameter validator enforces"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The LM kernel runs with fixed bounds of [0.5, 8] on both exponents, matching validateCoreyParams. The bounds are part of the problem definition, not derived from the data, they apply to both curves, and they are present whether or not the endpoint permeabilities are also being fitted.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 5, 'On the noise-free plant the 95 percent confidence interval for nw comes back as [2.4999999999999996, 2.4999999999999996]. Why is a zero-width interval the CORRECT output here?', '["The kernel always collapses intervals when it converges in fewer than ten iterations, whatever the residuals", "The interval width is scaled by the residual variance, which here sits at machine precision", "The bounds of 0.5 and 8 clamp the interval onto the estimate", "Confidence intervals are undefined for bounded parameters, so the kernel echoes the estimate at both ends"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The interval is built from the curvature at the solution scaled by the residual variance. With an rmsLog near 1e-16 the variance is effectively zero, so the width is zero: perfect data leaves nothing to be uncertain about. Iteration count does not collapse intervals, the parameter sits nowhere near its bounds, and bounded parameters still get curvature-based intervals.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 6, 'A five-point core table returns nw = 2.7 with 95 percent interval [1.9, 3.5]. What is the most defensible conclusion?', '["The rock is confirmed to be more water-wet than the Ekene sand", "The fit failed to converge and should be restarted from a different guess", "The table cannot distinguish rocks across that exponent range, and the efficient fix is more measurements in the low-kr decades where the exponent''s information lives", "The interval should be narrowed by refitting with fitEndpoints true"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A wide interval on a converged fit is a truthful statement about information content, not a failure: any exponent in [1.9, 3.5] is consistent with those five points. The exponent expresses itself most strongly in the near-endpoint decades, so extra points there shrink the interval fastest. Freeing two more parameters on five points widens uncertainty rather than narrowing it, and no wettability comparison survives an interval that wide.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 7, 'Two plugs report nw = 2.3 with interval [2.25, 2.35] and nw = 2.6 with interval [2.55, 2.65]. Two other plugs report the same point estimates but both with intervals spanning [1.8, 3.1]. What do the two situations support?', '["The first pair supports a real rock difference; the second supports only a statement about resolution", "Both pairs support the same conclusion, since the point estimates are identical and an interval cannot change an estimate", "Neither pair supports any conclusion until the endpoints are also fitted", "The second pair is the stronger evidence of heterogeneity because its intervals overlap"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Non-overlapping tight intervals mean the data resolves the two rocks apart. Heavily overlapping wide intervals mean the apparent difference is inside the noise, so writing it up as heterogeneity would be claiming what the data cannot show. Point estimates alone carry none of this distinction, and endpoint fitting is irrelevant to it.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 8, 'Setting fitEndpoints: true changes the fit in which specific way?', '["It frees Swc and Sor so the mobile saturation window can move with the exponents", "It reweights the residuals toward the endpoint rows, which the log objective otherwise underweights", "It replaces the log-space residuals with plain kr residuals near the ends", "The parameter vector grows from [nw, no] to [nw, no, krwMax, kroMax]"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The option frees the vertical scale of each curve: the endpoint permeabilities become bounded parameters starting from the table''s end values. The saturation window never moves, the residual definition is unchanged, and no reweighting occurs.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 9, 'With endpoints NOT fitted, where does the model''s krwMax come from?', '["It is fixed at the Corey validator''s default of 1.0", "It averages the top three water-curve rows", "It is taken directly from the table''s last row, the water value at residual oil", "The optimizer estimates it jointly with the exponents but reports only the exponents"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pinned fit reads krwMax from the table''s endpoint row (and kroMax from the first row): the measured end values ARE the model''s scale, and only the exponents are free. Nothing is averaged, nothing is silently estimated, and 1.0 is a bound, not a default.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 10, 'On the Ekene plant the free-endpoint fit returns rmsLog exactly 0 and the kernel reports the confidence-interval bounds as null. What does the null signify?', '["The fit diverged and the parameters should be discarded", "The variance scaling that builds the interval degenerates when the residual sum is a hard zero", "Null is the kernel''s encoding for an interval wider than the parameter bounds", "The intervals were suppressed because four free parameters exceed the information a thirteen-row table carries"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An error bar is a statement about residuals; with literally zero residuals the scaling that turns curvature into a width has nothing to work with, and the kernel returns null rather than invent a number. The parameters themselves are the exact plant, so nothing diverged, and the table''s 24 usable points determine four parameters comfortably.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 11, 'A table generated with nw = 1.2 and no = 3.5 is filed under a header claiming the Ekene standard exponents. The refit returns 1.2 and 3.5000000000000004 at rmsLog 1.1610990315520314e-16. What has been established?', '["The table is a clean Corey rock with a mislabelled header; the columns describe the rock", "The company standard should be updated to 1.2 and 3.5 for all sands", "The fit is biased toward low water exponents and needs a starting guess other than the neutral [2, 2]", "The table must be re-measured, because one table cannot be consistent with two different exponent sets"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A machine-precision refit that contradicts the header localizes the error to the label: the columns were generated by 1.2 and 3.5 and the fit recovered them exactly, converging in 5 iterations from the same neutral start of [2, 2]. One table has exactly one best Corey description, nothing about other sands follows, and a starting guess cannot manufacture a bias through an objective this smooth.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 12, 'A different table refits with rmsLog = 0.31. Following the module''s workflow, what is the honest next step?', '["Quote the fitted exponents with widened tolerances", "Rerun the fit with a smaller krFloor to admit more points", "Average the fitted exponents with the header''s claimed values and quote the mean as the rock''s shape", "Stop forcing a Corey shape and carry the measured points forward as a tabular kr specification"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An rmsLog of 0.31 means factor-of-two level mismatches somewhere on the curves: the rock is not Corey-shaped, so no exponent pair is a faithful summary and quoting one hides a shape error in every downstream number. The engine accepts tabular specifications natively. Admitting more near-floor points cannot fix a wrong shape, and averaging a bad fit with a header is not a method.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 13, 'The engine refuses to fit with the message that there are too few usable lab points above the kr floor. What triggers this refusal?', '["Fewer than fifteen rows in the table, whatever their kr values", "Fewer usable curve points than the parameter count plus two", "Any row with kr below 1e-4", "A table whose saturations were supplied unsorted and had to be reordered internally"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The guard compares the surviving residual targets against the size of the parameter vector plus two: below that, the fit is underdetermined and the engine refuses rather than return a meaningless answer. Individual sub-floor rows are simply excluded, row count alone is not the criterion, and unsorted tables are sorted internally.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 14, 'The pinned fit converged in 4 iterations from the neutral start [2, 2]; the planted 1.2/3.5 table converged in 5 despite the answer sitting much further from the start. What does this small difference illustrate?', '["The kernel caps iteration counts at the parameter dimension plus three", "Convergence speed is proportional to the distance between guess and answer", "The planted table is noisier than the Ekene grid", "The log-space objective is smooth enough that distance to the answer is cheap; iteration count reflects the objective''s shape, not the answer''s location"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Levenberg-Marquardt takes near-Newton steps on a smooth objective, so a well-conditioned problem converges in a handful of iterations almost regardless of where the answer sits within the bounds. Both tables are noise-free, no such cap exists, and the near-equality of 4 and 5 directly contradicts any proportionality to distance.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm01-fitting-the-lab', 15, 'Why do the fit''s residuals combine BOTH curves into one vector instead of fitting the water and oil curves separately?', '["Because one parameter vector must describe the whole rock at once, and shared structure such as the endpoints couples the two curves through the same minimization", "Because the oil curve alone lacks enough points to fit", "Because separate fits would require two different krFloor values", "Because the kernel only supports a single residual vector per call"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The deliverable is one consistent rock description, so both curves'' evidence must constrain the same parameters simultaneously; splitting the fit would let the two curves disagree about the rock they share. Each curve has ample points here, the floor applies per point regardless, and the kernel could be called twice if separate fits were actually wanted.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 1, 'In normalizeKrTable, what is each relative permeability curve divided by to produce the normalized table?', '["The target frame''s endpoint values, so the table is ready for the new rock", "The absolute permeability of the plug, which converts each effective permeability to its dimensionless form", "That curve''s own endpoint: krw by its value at 1 - Sor, kro by its value at Swc", "The larger of the two endpoint values, so both curves share one scale"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Normalization divides each curve by its own endpoint so every normalized table runs from (0, 0, 1) to (1, 1, 0). The target frame plays no role in normalization; it enters later, during scaling, and the absolute permeability never appears in a relative permeability table at all.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 2, 'The Ekene 13-row grid is normalized. What water value krwN does the middle row carry?', '["0.05303300858899109", "0.2499999999999999", "0.17677669529663698", "0.4871392896287463"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The middle row divides the stored krw 0.05303300858899109 by the endpoint 0.3, giving 0.17677669529663698, which is 0.5 to the power 2.5 up to a last-digit float wrinkle. 0.2499999999999999 is the oil curve''s normalized value at the same row, 0.05303300858899109 is the unnormalized krw, and 0.4871392896287463 is the water value at the upper quarter point.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 3, 'Why does a Corey exponent pass through normalization unchanged?', '["Because the normalized table stores the exponents separately in its endpoints object", "Because dividing krwMax times Swn to the power nw by krwMax leaves the bare power of Swn", "Because the engine refits the curve on the normalized axis using the same exponent bounds", "Because linear interpolation between rows reproduces any power law exactly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For a Corey curve the endpoint value is a pure multiplier, so dividing it out leaves Swn to the power nw with nothing else. No refit happens during normalization, the endpoints object stores saturations and endpoint kr values rather than exponents, and linear interpolation approximates a power law but never reproduces one exactly.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 4, 'On the Ekene frame with Swc 0.35 and Sor 0.25, what is the normalized saturation Swn at Sw 0.55?', '["0.5000000000000001", "0.30769230769230776", "0.26666666666666666", "0.55"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Swn divides the distance above connate by the mobile window 1 - Swc - Sor, which is 0.4, so (0.55 - 0.35)/0.4 lands at one half up to the float wrinkle. Dividing by 1 - Swc gives 0.30769230769230776 and dividing by 1 - Sor gives 0.26666666666666666: both are frame errors, and 0.55 is the raw saturation left untransformed.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 5, 'After scaleKrTable moves a measured curve set onto a new frame, which statement describes what survived the move?', '["The endpoint values survived the move; the exponents were refitted against the target rock''s own measurements", "Both the endpoints and the exponents survived; only the row count changed", "Nothing survived the move: the output is a fresh Corey set built from the target frame''s parameters alone", "The shape survived, exponents and curvature included; the frame was replaced by the target endpoints"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Scaling reads the source as pure shape on the normalized axis and rebuilds it inside the target frame, so curvature and exponents ride through while every endpoint is replaced. No fitting happens anywhere in the transfer, and the output honors the target frame exactly by construction.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 6, 'The Ekene grid is scaled onto a target frame with krwMax 0.45. What water value does the output carry at normalized saturation one half?', '["0.2124999999999999", "0.07954951288348665", "0.05303300858899109", "0.026516504294495546"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The normalized water value at the midpoint, 0.17677669529663698, multiplies the target endpoint 0.45 to give 0.07954951288348665. The value 0.2124999999999999 is the oil curve''s scaled counterpart at the same row, 0.05303300858899109 is the source value before any scaling, and 0.026516504294495546 is half the source value, an operation the transfer never performs.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 7, 'That same scaled pair is posted at which saturation on the target axis, for a target frame of Swc 0.25 and Sor 0.3?', '["0.625", "0.55", "0.5", "0.475"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The target span is 1 - 0.25 - 0.3 = 0.45, so normalized one half posts at 0.25 + 0.5 x 0.45 = 0.475. The source read the same shape point at 0.55, 0.5 is the normalized coordinate itself, and 0.625 comes from wrongly spanning with 1 - Swc instead of the mobile window.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 8, 'Reading the implied endpoints back from that scaled table returns Sor as 0.30000000000000004. What does the trailing 4 signify?', '["The transfer slightly shrank the mobile window and the table should be regenerated", "The engine enforces a minimum residual oil and rounded the input up", "A floating point artifact of computing 1 minus the last saturation 0.7, harmless once you name its arithmetic path", "Interpolation error inherited from the sparse source table"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The implied Sor is one minus the last row''s saturation, and 1 - 0.7 in binary floating point lands a sixteenth digit off the decimal value. The window is intact, no rounding rule exists, and interpolation error affects kr values between rows, never the endpoint bookkeeping.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 9, 'Two plugs read normalized water values of 0.1768 and 0.2500 at the normalized midpoint. Taking the midpoint as a screening read, what exponents do they imply?', '["About 2.5 for the first plug and 2 for the second, since the values are one half raised to those powers", "About 2 for the first plug and 2.5 for the second, since a higher normalized value must mean a higher exponent", "The same exponent for both plugs, with the difference between the readings explained by their endpoint values", "About 1.25 for both, splitting the difference between the two readings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One half to the power 2.5 is 0.17677669529663687 and one half squared is 0.25, so the lower midpoint value belongs to the higher exponent: stronger bowing pushes the curve down at mid saturation. Endpoints cannot explain any of it because normalization has already divided them out.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 10, 'Across a family of plugs whose normalized curves agree, what is the correct object to average when building the rock-type curve set?', '["The raw tables, row by row on the Sw axis", "The endpoints, taking the arithmetic mean of Swc and Sor across plugs", "The fitted exponents together with the averaged endpoints, giving one Corey set for the rock type", "The normalized shape, with endpoints mapped from logs or trends rather than averaged"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shape is the property the plugs demonstrated they share, so it is the thing entitled to an average. Endpoints ride on local porosity and texture and usually correlate with log properties, so averaging them manufactures a rock that exists nowhere. Averaging raw tables mixes frames and shapes together and inherits the worst of both.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 11, 'In the engine''s gas-oil Corey set, the normalized gas saturation Sgn divides Sg minus Sgc by which quantity?', '["1 - Swc, the total non-water space", "1 - Sgc, mirroring the oil-water convention", "1 - Swc - Sorg - Sgc, the mobile gas window", "1 - Swc - Sor, the same denominator the oil-water set uses"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mobile gas window excludes connate water, residual oil to gas, and the critical gas saturation, so all three subtract from one in the denominator. The oil-water denominator has no Sgc or Sorg in it, which is one structural sign that the two systems'' exponents are not interchangeable.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 12, 'A colleague argues that because normalized curves are dimensionless, the Ekene oil-water shape can fill a model''s gas-oil region. What is wrong with the argument?', '["Nothing, provided the gas-oil endpoints are installed during scaling", "Exponents belong to their fluid pair: gas is differently wetting, Sgc has no oil-water counterpart, and dimensionlessness does not make shape portable across systems", "The gas-oil system cannot be normalized because its window contains three saturations", "The transfer is valid only if the plug was measured with gas rather than mercury"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Normalization licenses comparison within a fluid system, never movement between systems, because the exponents encode how a specific fluid pair partitions the pores. The gas-oil window normalizes perfectly well through its own formula, and the lab fluid used for capillary pressure has nothing to do with relative permeability transfer.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 13, 'A drainage-branch curve shape is transferred into a waterflood model. Which transfer assumption does this break?', '["Same displacement direction: a waterflood is imbibition, and the engine models no hysteresis", "Same rock fabric, since drainage plugs are conventionally cut from a different facies than flood plugs", "Same wettability, since drainage experiments alter the surface chemistry", "None: the branches differ only in endpoints, which scaling replaces anyway"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drainage and imbibition are different curves on the same rock, and a waterflood runs on the imbibition branch. The engine believes whichever branch it is given, so the error propagates silently. Facies and wettability are separate assumptions, and the branches differ in shape as well as endpoints, so scaling does not repair the mistake.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 14, 'Which sentence makes a transferred curve set defensible in a report?', '["A statement that the curves were produced by the company''s standard normalization workflow and reviewed by the SCAL vendor", "A statement that the table passed validateKrTable with no errors", "A chain of custody: the source plug, the branch and fluid system, the endpoint provenance, and the shared-exponent evidence", "A statement that the curves match the simulation model''s existing rock types"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Each clause of the chain of custody names a checkable assumption and a way the curves could be wrong, which is what lets a reviewer price the uncertainty. Passing validation proves internal consistency, not physical relevance, and appeals to standard workflow or to agreement with the model assert nothing about the rock.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm02-normalization-and-transfer', 15, 'A one-plug SCAL program produces a beautiful normalized curve. What does the overlay test say about it?', '["The curve is confirmed, since it normalized cleanly onto the standard axis without complaint", "The exponents are reliable but the endpoints are not", "The curve should be rejected outright until a second plug has been cut from the same interval", "Nothing: every valid table normalizes, so with one plug there is a shape but no test of it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Normalizability is a property of all valid tables, so it carries no evidential weight. Agreement between independently measured plugs is what tests a shape, and with one plug that test cannot be run. That does not make the curve wrong or force a rejection; it makes the uncertainty undeclared until the report says the shape rests on a single sample.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 1, 'averageJCurves makes four moves: normalization onto Sw*, resampling onto a common 41-point grid, geometric-mean combination, and a power-law refit of the mean. On noise-free data, which move can bias the result?', '["The geometric mean, because it always understates the arithmetic mean of the same values", "The normalization, because dividing by 1 minus Swirr compresses the saturation axis", "The resample, because off-node values are interpolated and the interpolation has a systematic shape", "The refit, because Levenberg-Marquardt stops before machine convergence when the objective is flat near the optimum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Normalization, the mean, and the refit are arithmetic on the values they are handed and invent nothing. The resample must construct values between measured nodes, and its log-linear chord sits systematically above a convex log J curve, so exact inputs come out bent.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 2, 'One Ekene plug''s raw J points are handed directly to fitJPowerLaw with Swirr 0.25 supplied. What comes back?', '["a 0.25 and b 1 with r2Log exactly 1: the plant recovered to machine precision", "a 0.2491501585202375 and b 1.0102893566145976, the same drift the averaging pipeline shows", "a 0.25 and b 1, but with r2Log 0.9998442671274563 because the log residuals never fully close", "The fit refuses because a single plug gives too few positive J points to constrain two parameters"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The lab tables were generated from the designed curve through the engine''s own scaling, so the collapse back is exact and the direct fit on raw points recovers a 0.25 and b 1 with a perfect fit statistic. The drift numbers belong to the averaging pipeline, not the direct one.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 3, 'The full averaging pipeline runs on the three Ekene plugs with the true Swirr 0.25 shared. What value of a does the refit of the mean curve return?', '["0.25, since the plugs are noise-free and every step of the pipeline is exact", "0.2491501585202375", "0.9998442671274563", "1.0102893566145976"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The resample inflates every off-node point of the convex log J curve, worst at the toe, and the refit pays for the steepened toe by pulling a below the design value. 0.25 is the plant, not the pipeline''s output; the other two numbers are the refit''s r2Log and its b.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 4, 'In the averaged refit, b comes back at 1.0102893566145976 against a designed 1. Why does the drift push b UP?', '["The geometric mean of three identical curves is lower than each curve, which reads as a faster decline", "The shared Swirr of 0.25 compresses the reduced-saturation axis and steepens every sample equally", "The refit trades a against b, and since a fell, b had to rise to conserve the area under the curve", "Interpolation rides highest at the toe, and a high toe looks like a steeper climb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The chord error is largest where the curve bends hardest, which is the small-Sw* toe. A toe that reads high mimics a faster rise toward the asymptote, so the fitted exponent climbs, and the fit then pulls a down to stay near the exact node at Sw* equal to 1. Nothing about the mean of identical curves or the shared Swirr introduces any error at all.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 5, 'Between measured nodes, how does the tabulated J evaluator construct a value?', '["Linearly in log J against Sw*, which at a midpoint returns the geometric mean of the two node values", "Linearly in J against Sw*, which at a midpoint returns the plain arithmetic mean of the two node values", "Linearly in log J against log Sw*, which reproduces a power law between nodes exactly", "By evaluating the most recent power-law fit at the requested saturation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The evaluator interpolates log J linearly in Sw*. At a midpoint that is the average of two logs, which exponentiates to the geometric mean of the node values. Interpolating linearly in log Sw* would indeed be exact for a power law, which is precisely why the actual scheme, linear in Sw*, carries a bias.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 6, 'The first two resample nodes sit at Sw* 0.06666666666666665 and 0.1333333333333333 with J values 3.7500000000000004 and 1.8750000000000002. At the midpoint between them, the evaluator returns 2.6516504294495533. What does the designed curve give there?', '["2.8125000000000004", "3.7500000000000004", "2.5000000000000004", "2.6516504294495533"]'::jsonb, 2, 'With b equal to 1 the designed curve at Sw* just under 0.1 gives J of 2.5000000000000004, so the interpolated 2.6516504294495533 rides high by a factor of 1.0606601717798212. The value 2.8125 is the arithmetic mean of the two nodes, which is not what a log-linear chord returns, and repeating the interpolated value would make the chord error zero.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 7, 'The midpoint ride factor is 1.0606601717798212 near the toe but only 1.0029717747535971 near Sw* 0.4333333333333333. What sets the size of the chord error on a given node interval?', '["The number of plugs contributing values to that part of the common grid", "How hard log J bends across the interval, which for a reciprocal-shaped curve is greatest at the toe", "The absolute magnitude of J on the interval, since larger values necessarily carry larger absolute errors", "The local density of the 41-point grid, which thins out toward low reduced saturation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A chord approximates a curve with an error controlled by the curvature between its endpoints. Log J against Sw* bends hardest where Sw* is small and J climbs toward its asymptote, so the same 0.05-wide saturation step costs six percent at the toe and a third of a percent higher up. The grid spacing is uniform and plug count is identical everywhere.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 8, 'The averaged refit reports r2Log 0.9998442671274563 while both fitted parameters are wrong in the third digit. What is that statistic actually certifying?', '["That the resampled mean curve reproduces each plug''s raw J cloud within the min to max band", "That the three plugs collapsed to a single curve, confirming the Leverett scaling held", "That the fitted parameters lie within their own 95 percent confidence intervals", "That the mean curve handed to the fitter is very nearly a power law"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A fit statistic measures agreement between a model and the points the fitter was given. The resampled mean really is nearly a perfect power law, so r2Log is nearly 1, and the statistic is silent about the resample that biased those points before the fit saw them. It certifies the last step of the pipeline only.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 9, 'The Expert capstone grades avg_refit_a with expected 0.2491501585202375 and tolerance 0.0005. A candidate submits 0.25. What happens, and why is the tolerance set where it is?', '["It fails: the gap of 0.0008498414797624976 exceeds the tolerance, which was chosen so the design value and the pipeline value are distinguishable", "It passes: 0.25 is the designed curve and the grader accepts either legitimate chain within rounding", "It fails only if the candidate also submitted 1 for the fitted b, since the two fields are graded jointly", "It passes: the tolerance of 0.0005 is wider than the 0.34 percent relative drift"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The graded question is what the averaging pipeline returns, and 0.25 answers a different question. The gap between the chains is 0.0008498414797624976, which overshoots the 0.0005 tolerance by 0.00034984147976249757, so the design value fails by construction. Fields are graded independently, and 0.34 percent of 0.25 is larger than the tolerance, not smaller.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 10, 'A report states: the fitted a of the Ekene J curve is 0.249. According to this module''s provenance rule, what is wrong with that sentence?', '["The value is stale: the direct fit supersedes the averaged one whenever both chains exist", "It names no chain, and the quantity has two legitimate values depending on the pipeline", "Nothing, provided the value is quoted to at least twelve significant figures", "The value should be quoted with its r2Log so a reader can judge the fit quality"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both 0.25 by the direct chain and 0.2491501585202375 by the averaging chain are correct answers to their own questions, so a bare number is ambiguous in exactly the way that costs colleagues an afternoon. Precision and fit statistics do not resolve which question was asked; the provenance statement does.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 11, 'Under what data conditions does the averaging pipeline give a BETTER reservoir curve than any direct single-plug fit?', '["Whenever at least three plugs are available, since the geometric mean needs that many to form a band", "Never: the resample bias is always present, so the direct fit is always at least as good", "When the scatter between plugs is large relative to the resample bias, so the noise suppressed exceeds the bias introduced", "When the plugs were measured with different lab fluid systems, since averaging cancels the sigma cos theta factors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Averaging suppresses independent scatter but adds a fixed resample bias. With noisy plugs the trade is favorable; on the noise-free fixture it is pure loss. The fluid-system factors are removed by the J transform itself before any averaging, and the band is descriptive, not the reason to average.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 12, 'The height-averaged water saturation of the Ekene crest column, by the graded definition, is:', '["0.1334503339494001", "0.35062979402484734", "0.35, since the booking''s flat saturation was calibrated to this column", "0.48345033394940007"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 2000-interval trapezoid of Sw against height from the contact to the crest gives 0.48345033394940007. The value 0.35062979402484734 is the saturation at the single crest elevation, 0.1334503339494001 is the difference between the column average and the flat booking, and the flat 0.35 is what the average is being compared against.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 13, 'The graded column average integrates Sw(h) by composite trapezoid with 2000 intervals. Between which limits does the integral run?', '["From the free water level at h 0 up to the crest, so the wet rock below the mapped contact is included in the average", "From the contact, at 10.31162356877775 ft above the free water level, up to the crest at 76.85316015526888 ft", "From the base of the transition zone where Sw is 0.9 up to the crest", "From the contact up to the elevation where Sw first reaches 0.36"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column of interest is the oil column: contact to crest. Starting at the free water level instead pads the average with rock the model holds at Sw 1 and changes the answer, which is why the bounds are part of the graded definition. The 0.9 and 0.36 elevations are ladder waypoints inside the column, not its limits.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 14, 'Having computed the crest column''s average saturation, why does this course refuse to state the field-wide average for the 169 booked cells?', '["It has not been derived: each column has its own height and no such calculation exists", "Because the other columns are taller than the crest column and would dominate the average", "Because the trapezoid rule loses accuracy when applied to columns shorter than about 10 m", "Because the field average is confidential to the volumetrics course and out of scope here"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The scope of a calculation is part of the calculation: one column was integrated, so one column''s average exists. The crest column is the tallest, not the shortest, and shorter columns sit wetter, but by how much per cell is exactly what has not been computed. Refusing to extrapolate is the point, not a formality.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm03-the-average-that-drifts', 15, 'The column average 0.48345033394940007 sits 0.1334503339494001 above the flat booking''s 0.35. What does that difference mean for the oil in the crest column?', '["The column holds no recoverable oil, since the average saturation exceeds the kr table''s connate value", "The booking understates the oil, because a wetter average implies a thicker transition zone was ignored", "The average oil fraction is 0.5165496660505999 rather than 0.65, so the column holds roughly four fifths of what the flat booking implies", "Nothing changes, because saturation averages do not commute with volume calculations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Oil fraction is one minus water saturation, so the physics-weighted column carries 0.5165496660505999 against the booking''s 0.65, roughly a four-fifths ratio for this one column. Wetter means less oil, not more, and a saturation above connate describes water content, not producibility of the column as a whole.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 1, 'With dip switched on, how does the gravity term enter the engine''s fractional flow expression?', '["The denominator gains a term G times krw, which raises the total mobility and flattens the whole curve", "The numerator''s 1 becomes 1 minus G times kro; the mobility sum in the denominator is unchanged", "The horizontal fw is multiplied through by 1 minus G, so every saturation is scaled by the same factor", "The saturation axis is shifted by G before the mobility ratio is formed, so the curve slides sideways"]'::jsonb, 1, 'All of the dip physics sits in one product, G times kro, subtracted from the numerator. Because the correction is proportional to the oil relative permeability it is largest where oil flows best and dies away where kro decays, so the curve deforms at its low saturation toe rather than sliding or rescaling uniformly. Nothing is added to the mobility sum.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 2, 'GRAV_CONST is 0.001127 times 0.433, which multiplies out to 0.00048799099999999996. What are those two factors doing?', '["0.001127 is the water gradient in psi per foot and 0.433 converts millidarcies to darcies", "0.001127 is the Darcy constant in SI units and 0.433 rescales the area from square feet to square metres", "0.001127 is the field-unit Darcy constant and 0.433 psi per foot turns a specific gravity into a pressure gradient", "0.001127 converts reservoir barrels per day to cubic feet per second and 0.433 is the sine of the reference dip"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gravity number is a ratio of a gravity-driven flux to the imposed viscous flux, so it needs the same Darcy constant the rest of the field-unit flow equations use, and it needs a way to turn the dimensionless density contrast into a real gradient. The pair 0.001127 and 0.433 does exactly that, which is why permeability arrives in millidarcies and area in square feet without any further conversion.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 3, 'The designed case is k 250 md, A 20000 ft2, qt 2000 rb/d, dip 10 degrees updip, gammaW 1.03, gammaO 0.8654434250764526, muO 1.8 cp. What gravity coefficient does the engine return?', '["0.16455657492354747", "0.004841777122376944", "0.019367108489507776", "0.0774684339580311"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The chain is 0.00048799099999999996 times 250 times 20000 times the contrast 0.16455657492354747 times sin(10 deg), all divided by muO times qt, which is 3600. That returns 0.019367108489507776. The contrast itself is only one factor of the numerator, while 0.004841777122376944 and 0.0774684339580311 are the same geometry run at 8000 and 500 rb/d.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 4, 'The designed case is run updip at 10 degrees. What is the displacement efficiency at breakthrough?', '["0.5090537496023166", "0.5081700834294871", "0.5088773453049006", "0.5095807170488317"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gravity assist reduces water fractional flow at the toe, the tangency moves out to Swf 0.6376, and EDbt rises to 0.5095807170488317 from the flat case''s 0.5088773453049006. The value 0.5081700834294871 belongs to the mirrored downdip run and 0.5090537496023166 to the same updip geometry pushed at 8000 rb/d.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 5, 'Switching the designed dip on moves Swf from 0.6372 to 0.6376 and EDbt from 0.5088773453049006 to 0.5095807170488317. Which reported quantity comes back identical in the two runs?', '["EDmax, still 0.6153846153846154, since the ceiling is fixed by the endpoints and gravity moves no endpoint", "fwf, still 0.8682763300877854, since the fractional flow at the front is fixed by the mobility ratio alone", "QiBt, still 0.33077027444818546, since throughput to breakthrough follows the mobility ratio and not the curve", "Breakthrough time in days, since dip redistributes efficiency within a fixed pore volume throughput"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EDmax is (1 - Swc - Sor) divided by (1 - Swc), a pure endpoint statement, and the dip term touches neither Swc nor Sor. Everything read off the corrected curve does move: fwf goes to 0.8682854818839988 and QiBt to 0.33122746608174053, and because QiBt moves the days to breakthrough move with it.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 6, 'A marginal forecast on an Ekene-like flood is presented and someone proposes crediting the structure''s 10 degrees of dip to close the gap. At field rate on a favorable mobility ratio, what is the honest response?', '["Credit it: 0.5095807170488317 against 0.5088773453049006 is a gain of about 1.4 percent of the flat efficiency", "Recompute at 500 rb/d and book EDbt 0.511665846741284, a gravity gain large enough to close a marginal case", "Refuse on principle, because the engine treats dip only as a screening flag and returns no efficiency for it", "The dip term is worth about 0.0007 of EDbt here, so a case that only works with it does not work"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The gain is real and it is 0.0007 of displacement efficiency, which is roughly 0.14 percent of the flat value, not 1.4 percent. Dropping to 500 rb/d buys 0.0026 more, still far too little to rescue economics and paid for with a flood sixteen times slower. The term is quantitative and it is honest, and it is the right tool for ranking geometries rather than for closing gaps.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 7, 'G is 0.0774684339580311 at qt 500 and 0.019367108489507776 at qt 2000 on the same geometry. How should the ratio of exactly four be read?', '["As a coincidence of this geometry, since the ratio also depends on where the tangency lands", "As arithmetic: rate is the only changed input and G scales as 1 over qt, with no modelling in between", "As approximate only, because the front saturation moved between the two runs and feeds back into the coefficient", "As evidence that the engine re-derives G from the corrected curve each time it is called"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gravity number is assembled from six inputs before any curve is built, so it knows nothing about the front, the tangency, or the corrected fw. Rate enters once, in the denominator, and quartering it quadruples G exactly. The modelling happens downstream, in what the enlarged coefficient does to the shape of the curve.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 8, 'Cutting the designed flood from qt 8000 to qt 500 rb/d moves EDbt from 0.5090537496023166 to 0.511665846741284. What has been bought, and at what price?', '["About 0.026 of displacement efficiency, at no cost beyond deferring some of the production into later years", "A move up to the ceiling EDmax 0.6153846153846154, which gravity-assisted floods reach once G exceeds 0.05", "About 0.0026 of efficiency, for a flood sixteen times as slow to the same throughput", "Nothing measurable, since both values round to 0.51 and the gap is smaller than the Welge scan grid step"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A factor of sixteen in rate buys about a quarter of a saturation point at breakthrough, and buys it with calendar time, since the same pore volume throughput now takes sixteen times as long. The gap is a genuine 0.0026 and not a grid artifact, and no rate lifts EDbt to EDmax, which is an endpoint ceiling reached only after long post-breakthrough throughput.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 9, 'At qt 8000 the run reports Swf 0.6372, exactly the flat-case front, while EDbt reads 0.5090537496023166 against the flat 0.5088773453049006. What does that combination show?', '["The engine reverts to the flat construction once the gravity number falls below a hard cutoff of 0.005", "Gravity has switched off at this rate, and the efficiency difference is left over from the previous case", "EDbt and Swf are read from different curves, so the two are not expected to agree about small changes", "The front is reported on the 0.0004 scan grid, so a small correction can fail to move it while efficiency still carries it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'G at 8000 rb/d is 0.004841777122376944, small but not zero, and it deforms the same single curve both quantities come from. Swf is quantized by the 1000-point Welge scan over the mobile range, so a correction that does not clear the next grid point leaves the reported front unchanged, while EDbt varies continuously. When you need to detect a small effect, read EDbt rather than Swf.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 10, 'The designed case is rerun with the dip set to -10 degrees. Which pair does the engine return?', '["G -0.019367108489507776 with EDbt 0.5095807170488317", "G -0.019367108489507776 with EDbt 0.5081700834294871", "G -0.004841777122376944 with EDbt 0.5088773453049006", "G 0.019367108489507776 with EDbt 0.5081700834294871"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sine carries the whole reversal, so the coefficient is the exact negative of the updip value. With G negative the numerator becomes 1 plus its magnitude times kro, water fractional flow is enhanced wherever oil still flows, the front weakens to Swf 0.6368, and efficiency at breakthrough falls to 0.5081700834294871.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 11, 'A study computes the gravity magnitude correctly but takes the sign from where the injector sits on the map instead of from the direction the front travels, so it reports the updip efficiency where the downdip one belongs. How far out is that against the graded tolerance of 0.0005?', '["0.0007, just outside the tolerance", "0.0014, close to three times the tolerance", "0.0005, exactly at the tolerance and therefore a pass", "0.019367108489507776, the gravity number itself"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two dipped values are 0.5095807170488317 and 0.5081700834294871, so a sign error spans both gaps at once rather than one of them. The 0.0007 figure is the distance from either dipped value to the flat one, which is the error made by ignoring dip altogether, and it already fails the same tolerance.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 12, 'G is mirrored exactly between the +10 and -10 runs, yet the updip gain in EDbt and the downdip loss agree only to the first significant figure. What accounts for the asymmetry?', '["sin(+10) and sin(-10) differ in magnitude in the last bits of double precision arithmetic", "The clamp at zero fractional flow trims the downdip curve and not the updip one", "The flat reference is computed on a finer saturation grid than either of the dipped cases", "The tangency point migrates as the curve deforms, so the construction is nonlinear"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A mirrored input does not force a mirrored output when the output comes from a construction that reoptimizes. The secant from connate water finds its steepest saturation somewhere new on each deformed curve, and efficiency at breakthrough is then read at that new point, so the two responses are near twins rather than twins. The clamp only ever acts on strong assist, which is the updip direction.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 13, 'When part of the sampled curve above connate water sits at exactly zero fractional flow, what warning does analyzeDisplacement attach?', '["\"Negative fractional flow was clamped to zero; raise the total rate or reduce the dip before using this result.\"", "\"Displacement is gravity-stable; the Welge construction has been skipped below the front saturation.\"", "\"Gravity assist is strong enough to hold fw at zero over part of the saturation range (gravity-stable displacement).\"", "\"The gravity term dominates the viscous term; efficiencies are reported outside the model''s stable range.\""]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine floors a raw negative fractional flow at zero, because negative water flow means nothing in a one dimensional immiscible model, and it flags the case rather than silently returning the clamped curve. The construction still runs and the efficiencies are still reported, so any wording claiming a skipped or suppressed calculation misreads what the flag does.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 14, 'As G grows, where on the saturation axis does the clamped zero region open first?', '["At the front, where the tangent construction is most sensitive to any change in the curve", "Uniformly across the mobile range, since the gravity coefficient itself carries no saturation dependence", "At residual oil, where kro falls to zero and the corrected numerator collapses with it", "Just above connate water, where kro is near its endpoint so the product G times kro reaches 1 first"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The clamp condition is G times kro at least 1, and kro is a decreasing function of water saturation, so the inequality is met at the wettest-oil end of the range first and the zero region then grows upward as G grows. At residual oil kro is zero, which makes the correction vanish rather than dominate. The coefficient is saturation-independent but the product it forms is not.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm04-gravity-and-the-dip-term', 15, 'A prospect screens gravity-stable on this engine. What does the flag entitle you to say?', '["That the inputs have left the viscous regime this model describes, and the case now needs a study with vertical resolution", "That the front is stable, since the flag is raised by the engine''s Dietz stability check on the dipping interface", "That the critical rate for gravity segregation has been computed and the proposed rate sits below it", "That water will not be produced below the clamped saturations, which is what the zero region forecasts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Behind the flag there is no segregated flow model, no stability criterion, no critical rate correlation and no vertical equilibrium calculation. It reports one arithmetic fact, that the clamp fired, and the honest use of that fact is to say the operating point sits outside the assumptions and to hand the case to a tool with vertical resolution. A clamped region is a boundary marker, not a production forecast.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 1, 'A displacement spec carries polymerMuMult 4. What does the engine do with that number?', '["It multiplies both viscosities by 4 so the ratio is preserved", "It raises krwMax by a factor of 4 to represent the thickened bank", "It multiplies the water viscosity alone by 4 before building fw", "It divides the oil viscosity by 4, which is algebraically equivalent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The multiplier is applied to muW at the door of makeFwFunction and nowhere else: muWeff = muW x polymerMuMult. The oil viscosity and the rel perm set are untouched. Dividing muO by the same factor would preserve the viscosity ratio inside fw but would corrupt every quantity that uses muO alone, and multiplying both viscosities would change nothing at all.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 2, 'With Ekene water at 0.5 cp and polymerMuMult 4, what effective water viscosity does analyzeDisplacement report?', '["2 cp", "4 cp", "0.125 cp", "1.8 cp"]'::jsonb, 0, 'muWeff = 0.5 x 4 = 2 cp. Reading the multiplier itself as the viscosity is the common slip; 4 is dimensionless. Dividing instead of multiplying gives 0.125 cp, and 1.8 cp is the oil viscosity, which the polymer knob never touches.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 3, 'The base Ekene flood runs at an endpoint mobility ratio of 1.2. At polymerMuMult 4 the ratio becomes:', '["4.8", "0.3", "1.2, unchanged, because M uses endpoint permeabilities only", "0.6153846153846154"]'::jsonb, 1, 'M = (krwMax/muWeff)/(kroMax/muO) = (0.3/2)/(0.9/1.8) = 0.3. Because muWeff sits alone in the water mobility''s denominator, M falls by exactly the multiplier. Multiplying M by 4 instead gives 4.8, the sign of pushing the lever the wrong way; M does contain the viscosities, and 0.6153846153846154 is the displacement ceiling, a different quantity entirely.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 4, 'Which front saturation does the Welge construction return for the polymer case at multiplier 4?', '["0.6372", "0.7251777184221064", "0.75", "0.7044"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The thickened water pushes the whole fw curve down and to the right, so the tangency point climbs from the base 0.6372 to 0.7044. The value 0.7251777184221064 is the average saturation behind the polymer front, not the front itself, and 0.75 is 1 minus Sor, the residual endpoint the front never quite reaches.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 5, 'Breakthrough for the polymer flood arrives at how many pore volumes injected?', '["0.33077027444818546", "0.3751777184221064", "0.7251777184221064", "2.665403489860014"]'::jsonb, 1, 'QiBt is the reciprocal of the front tangent slope: 1/2.665403489860014 = 0.3751777184221064 pore volumes, later than the base flood''s 0.33077027444818546. The slope itself is not a pore volume count, and 0.7251777184221064 is the average saturation behind the front wearing the wrong units.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 6, 'Sweeping the polymer multiplier from 1 to 6 in the design explorer, one tile never moves. Which quantity is it?', '["The fractional flow at the front", "The pore volumes injected at breakthrough", "The ultimate displacement efficiency EDmax", "The front saturation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EDmax = (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) contains no viscosity, so no viscosity knob can move it; it stays at 0.6153846153846154 in every case this course runs. The front saturation, the front''s fractional flow, and the breakthrough pore volumes all shift with the multiplier because they live on the fw curve.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 7, 'Why exactly does polymer leave EDmax untouched?', '["Because the ceiling is set by the endpoints Swc and Sor, and the multiplier touches neither", "Because the engine clamps the ceiling to its base value whenever the polymer multiplier is active", "Because the gains before and after breakthrough cancel", "Because adsorption removes the extra recovery in the field"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ceiling is pure endpoint bookkeeping: the movable window (1 - Sor - Swc) over the flushable window (1 - Swc). Polymer changes the path to the ceiling, not the walls. There is no clamp in the engine, no cancellation to invoke, and adsorption is a real-world effect deliberately outside this screening model, not the reason the model''s own ceiling is fixed.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 8, 'How large is the polymer flood''s improvement in displacement efficiency at breakthrough over the base Ekene flood?', '["About 0.15", "About 0.023", "About 0.0007", "About 0.068"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EDbt rises from 0.5088773453049006 to 0.5771964898801638, a gain of about 0.068, close to seven saturation points of the column delivered before water arrives. The few-ten-thousandths figure is the scale of the gravity trim on this field, an order of magnitude and a half smaller, which is why viscosity is the big lever.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 9, 'The polymer front sits at 0.7044 and breakthrough comes at 0.3751777184221064 PV. The average water saturation behind the front at breakthrough is:', '["0.7251777184221064, from Swc plus the breakthrough pore volumes", "0.7044, the same as the front in a sharpened flood", "0.6807702744481854", "0.9446, the fractional flow read as a saturation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Welge identity SwAvgBt = Swc + QiBt gives 0.35 + 0.3751777184221064 = 0.7251777184221064, and the average always exceeds the front because the region behind the shock keeps draining oil. The value 0.6807702744481854 is the base flood''s average, and reading the front''s fractional flow as a saturation confuses two different axes of the same plot.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 10, 'The front tangent slope falls from 3.023246274678918 in the base case to 2.665403489860014 with polymer. What does the shallower tangent tell you?', '["Water takes a smaller share of the total flow at the front than it did in the base case", "Breakthrough arrives later, since PV at breakthrough is the reciprocal of the slope", "The displacement ceiling has risen", "The front is moving faster through the rock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'QiBt = 1/fwPrimeF, so a shallower tangent means more pore volumes before breakthrough: 1/2.6654... = 0.3751... against 1/3.0232... = 0.3307.... The front''s fractional flow actually rises to 0.944618996806389, the ceiling never moves, and the shock velocity scales with the slope, so a shallower tangent means a slower front, not a faster one.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 11, 'According to the engine''s own polymer warning, which single effect IS represented in a polymer screening run?', '["Polymer adsorption onto the rock", "Permeability reduction behind the slug", "Shear dependent rheology of the solution", "Multiplication of the water viscosity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The warning states it exactly: water viscosity multiplied for screening only; no adsorption, permeability reduction, or rheology effects. The one mechanism inside the model is the viscosity multiplier; the three named omissions are the studies a real project buys next.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 12, 'Relative to the screening estimate, which way does polymer adsorption push a real flood''s gain?', '["Above the screening estimate, because adsorbed polymer keeps working after injection stops", "Below the screening estimate, because the advancing bank loses viscosity to the rock", "It leaves performance unchanged and only raises chemical cost", "It reverses the sign of the gain, making polymer floods recover less than plain water"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Adsorption strips polymer from the leading edge of the bank, so the viscosity that reaches the front is lower than the viscosity you injected, and the real gain lands below the constant-multiplier screening gain. That is what makes the screening case an optimistic bound with a known direction. It does cost money too, but not only money, and it does not reverse the sign of a healthy gain.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 13, 'A screening run on another field shows polymer improving breakthrough efficiency by only 0.011. The Expert reading of that number is:', '["Decline: the omitted mechanisms push the real gain below an already marginal screening gain", "Proceed: screening models are conservative, so the field will beat 0.011", "Raise the multiplier in the model until the gain clears the threshold", "Rerun the screening at finer saturation resolution to recover the missing efficiency"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The screening case is an optimistic bound: adsorption, in particular, only ever erodes the modelled gain. A marginal screening answer is therefore a no, not a maybe. Turning the multiplier knob changes the question rather than the field, and numerical resolution is not where the missing efficiency lives.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 14, 'On which reservoir would the dip and rate levers plausibly rival the viscosity lever, instead of trailing it by orders of magnitude as on Ekene?', '["A steeply dipping, very permeable reservoir flooded slowly", "A low relief, moderate permeability sand flooded at commercial rates", "Any reservoir with heavy oil, regardless of structure", "A thin sand flooded as fast as injectivity allows"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gravity term scales with k sin(alpha)/qt: steep dip and high permeability grow it, and a slow flood declines to erase it. Ekene fails all three tests, which is why its gravity trim is worth only a few ten-thousandths. Heavy oil raises the stakes for the viscosity lever, not the gravity one, and flooding fast is precisely how the gravity term is destroyed.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm05-polymer-and-the-design-space', 15, 'In this module''s viscosity ranking, the 10 cp oil case reaches a breakthrough displacement efficiency of:', '["0.4299040270894154", "0.5088773453049006", "0.3693282019820123", "0.5771964898801638"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At 10 cp the mobility ratio is 6.666666666666667 and EDbt collapses to 0.3693282019820123. The 5 cp case sits at 0.4299040270894154, the 1.8 cp base at 0.5088773453049006, and the polymer case at 0.5771964898801638: a twenty-point spread across the viscosity axis, which is the whole argument for calling it the big lever.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 1, 'Across the whole Expert tier, which change moves the ultimate displacement efficiency EDmax off its base value of 0.6153846153846154?', '["Reducing the residual oil saturation in the kr endpoint set", "Multiplying water viscosity by four through the polymer screening multiplier", "Reversing the dip of the designed gravity case from plus 10 to minus 10 degrees", "Cutting the total rate from 2000 rb/d to 500 rb/d so the gravity coefficient quadruples"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EDmax is the closed form (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc), a function of the endpoints alone. Dip, rate, and polymer all reshape the fractional flow curve and move the front and the breakthrough efficiency, but none of them touches Swc or Sor, so the ceiling stays exactly where the endpoints put it.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 2, 'The averaged-refit capstone field expects 0.2491501585202375 at a tolerance of 0.0005. A learner submits the design value 0.25. What happens, and why?', '["It passes, because 0.25 is the true value of the underlying curve", "It fails: the field grades the workflow''s actual output and the drift of 0.00085 exceeds the tolerance", "It fails, because the refit is quoted on the Sw* axis where a has different units", "It passes, because the tolerance is applied to the ratio of the two values rather than their difference"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field was designed so that the plant fails: the resampling bias moves a by 0.00085, and the tolerance of 0.0005 sits inside that gap. The question being graded is whether you ran averageJCurves and read its result, not whether you remember the design constant.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 3, 'A learner submits the flat-case breakthrough efficiency 0.5088773453049006 for the updip gravity field. The graded value is 0.5095807170488317 at tolerance 0.0005. What does the grader conclude?', '["The answer passes, since both values round to 0.509", "The answer fails on a rounding error in the fifth decimal", "The answer fails because a horizontal-case computation was submitted for a dipping case", "The answer passes, because the dip term is negligible at field rate and inside the stated tolerance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gap between flat and updip is about 0.0007, deliberately wider than the 0.0005 tolerance. The submission is not imprecise; it is the answer to a different case, the horizontal displacement, and the tolerance was chosen so that wrong-case answers fail even though the physical effect is small.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 4, 'A learner''s two dip answers come back swapped: the updip field holds 0.5081700834294871 and the downdip field holds 0.5095807170488317. What single error explains both?', '["The rate was doubled in one case and halved in the other", "The polymer multiplier was left switched on for one of the two runs and off for the other", "The trapezoid interval count was set to 1000 instead of 2000 in one of the two runs", "The sign of the dip angle was reversed, flipping which case is displacement updip"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two values are exactly the updip and downdip results exchanged, and the only lever that exchanges them is the sign of sin(alpha) in the gravity term. In this convention displacement updip is positive dip; reverse the sign and every case lands in the other field.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 5, 'Which designed case produces the breakthrough efficiency 0.5771964898801638?', '["The gravity case run at 500 rb/d, where the gravity coefficient reaches its largest designed value", "The polymer screening case, water viscosity multiplied by four on the base Ekene displacement", "The base Ekene displacement evaluated at one full pore volume injected", "The reduced-residual sensitivity with Sor cut to 0.15"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The polymer case drops the endpoint mobility ratio to 0.3 and lifts the front to 0.7044, banking 0.5771964898801638 of the movable-oil ceiling by breakthrough. The slow gravity case reaches only 0.511665846741284, and the Sor 0.15 case changes the ceiling itself, which the polymer case never does.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 6, 'The averaged J curve refits with r2Log 0.9998442671274563 while both parameters are biased off the plant. What is the correct reading of that fit statistic?', '["It proves the bias must come from noise in the lab data", "It shows the refit failed to converge and the parameters are meaningless", "It is evidence the Swirr override was set to the wrong value", "A near-perfect fit statistic measures internal consistency, and a workflow can be internally consistent and systematically biased at once"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The refit describes the resampled mean curve almost perfectly; the problem is that the resampled curve itself sits off the plant. A fit statistic audits the last step of a workflow, never the steps before it, so a biased pipeline can carry a spotless r2 all the way to the answer.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 7, 'In the four moves of averageJCurves, where does the parameter drift enter?', '["In the resampling of each plug through its log-linear tabulated evaluator", "In the geometric mean, which sits below the arithmetic mean and underweights the largest J values", "In the shared Swirr override, which compresses the Sw* axis", "In the min/max band, which clips the outlying samples before the mean is formed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Normalization, the geometric mean, and the refit are exact operations here. The one inexact move is evaluating each plug on the common grid through log-linear interpolation: a chord under a curve that is convex in log space sits systematically off it, and that offset is what the refit then faithfully fits.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 8, 'On the 13-row lab grid, the fit returns nw 2.4999999999999996 and no 2. A learner reports the water exponent as 2. What went wrong?', '["Nothing; at the 0.001 tolerance the two exponents are interchangeable", "The fit was run with endpoints freed, which reorders the output", "The exponents were read from the wrong curves: nw governs krw and no governs kro", "The krFloor excluded the rows that determine nw, so the fit could not resolve it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exponents are not symmetric: krw rises as krwMax times Swn to the power nw, while kro falls as kroMax times (1 - Swn) to the power no. Reading 2 for the water exponent means the oil exponent''s slot was transcribed. The tolerance of 0.001 separates 2 from 2.5 by a factor of five hundred.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 9, 'The Corey fit on the 26 curve-points of the 13-row grid reports pointsUsed 24. Why?', '["Two rows fell outside the mobile saturation window and were clipped", "The LM kernel discards the two highest-leverage points on each curve as a robustness step", "Duplicate saturations in the grid collapsed two rows into their neighbours during the sort", "One krw and one kro value sit at or below the 1e-4 floor, unusable in log space"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The residuals are log10 mismatches, and the endpoint zeros, krw at connate and kro at residual, are definitional rather than informative, so each curve loses exactly its zero-end row to the floor. Twenty-six minus two is the twenty-four the fit reports.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 10, 'What does the graded column average 0.48345033394940007 claim, and what does it deliberately not claim?', '["It claims the crest cell''s booking saturation and not the transition-zone profile", "It claims the crest column''s height-averaged saturation, not the field average", "It claims the field-wide average saturation and not the value of any single column", "It claims the free-water-level saturation and not the crest value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The integral runs over one column, from the contact to the crest, by the stated trapezoid rule. Smaller columns elsewhere in the field are wetter still, but the course never derives the field-wide number, so the honest claim stops at the column the case states.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 11, 'Among the designed gravity runs, which rate delivers the largest efficiency gain at breakthrough, and what is the general rule?', '["500 rb/d, because the gravity coefficient scales as one over the total rate", "8000 rb/d, because a faster flood sweeps more of the section", "2000 rb/d, because the designed case is tuned to the field''s actual injection rate", "All rates gain equally, because the dip angle is identical in every one of the runs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The coefficient carries qt in its denominator, so slowing the flood from 2000 to 500 rb/d quadruples it and lifts the breakthrough efficiency to 0.511665846741284. At 8000 rb/d the term is nearly erased and the front returns to the flat grid point. Slow floods harvest gravity; fast floods buy time instead.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 12, 'Why does the capstone prompt specify composite trapezoid with 2000 intervals for the column average rather than just asking for the integral?', '["Because the engine cannot evaluate the saturation profile at more than 2000 points in one pass", "Because Simpson''s rule diverges on saturation-height profiles", "Because the rule is part of the case: a different rule or step lands on a different number", "Because 2000 intervals guarantees the answer is exact to machine precision"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A graded numerical integral is only reproducible if everyone integrates the same way. Stating the rule and the interval count makes the field a single well-defined computation, the same discipline as stating which J chain or which dip sign a number came from.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 13, 'Which statement correctly describes the platform''s dependency locks around displacement math?', '["The SCAL module defines its own Corey primitives and the fractional-flow module imports them", "The waterflood engine re-implements Buckley-Leverett to avoid a cross-domain import", "Each course vendors its own copy of the displacement engine to stay independent", "Corey primitives are defined once in the fractional-flow module; the SCAL module imports them, and the waterflood engine imports the displacement analysis as its one sanctioned cross-domain import"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The direction is locked: one definition of the primitives, imported by SCAL, and one Buckley-Leverett construction, imported by waterflood. Duplicate implementations of the same primitive drift apart silently, which is the code-level version of the provenance failures this tier kept measuring.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 14, 'The Ekene J curve was built from drainage data while the kr set is an imbibition set. What does the branch discipline require when predicting the waterflood?', '["Average the two branches, since the truth lies between them", "Use the imbibition kr set for the flood and keep the drainage J curve for charging and initial saturations, naming the branch each time", "Convert the drainage curve to imbibition through the J-function before use", "Prefer the drainage curve, because it was measured at higher capillary pressure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With no hysteresis model, the branches cannot be converted into each other; each describes its own direction of saturation change. The flood is imbibition, the charge was drainage, and the honest workflow states which branch every curve belongs to rather than blending them.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'module', 'm06-the-expert-reading', 15, 'Several Expert tolerances sit just inside the gap to the nearest plausible wrong answer. What are these tolerances designed to test?', '["How many significant figures the learner can transcribe without error", "Whether the learner''s calculator carries full double precision", "Whether the number came from the stated computation rather than a neighbouring method", "Whether the learner rounded at the final step or at the intermediate steps along the way"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The plant fails the drift field, the flat efficiency fails both dip fields, and a swapped sign fails them in pairs. Each tolerance was placed after measuring the nearest wrong-method value, so passing certifies the method, not the transcription.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 1, 'fitCoreyToKrTable builds its residuals as joint log10 mismatches over both curves rather than linear differences. What does this buy the fit?', '["A guarantee that the optimizer converges within its 60-iteration cap for any physically valid table.", "The ability to include negative kr values that linear residuals would reject.", "The near-endpoint decades, where kr is small, carry weight rather than being drowned out.", "Freedom from parameter bounds, since logarithms keep the exponents finite."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Relative permeability spans decades. In linear space the few high-kr points dominate the sum of squares and the curvature near the endpoints, which carries the exponent information, is ignored. Logs weight every usable decade evenly. Convergence, negative values and bounds are unrelated to the choice.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 2, 'A lab row carries krw = 0 at connate water. What does the engine''s krFloor of 1e-4 do with it?', '["Excludes it from the water curve''s residuals only; endpoint zeros are definitional anyway.", "Rounds it up to the floor value of 1e-4 and keeps it in the water-curve residual set.", "Drops the entire row, removing its kro value from the oil curve''s residuals as well.", "Raises a validation error immediately, because tables containing hard zeros cannot be fitted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The log of a hard zero is unusable, and the zero at the endpoint is true by definition rather than being information about the exponent. The row''s kro value stays in play for the oil curve; nothing is rounded and nothing errors.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 3, 'The 13-row Ekene lab grid offers 26 curve-points, yet the fixed-endpoint fit reports pointsUsed 24. Where did the two points go?', '["The two endpoint saturations are merged into a single anchor row before fitting.", "One krw row and one kro row sit at or below the 1e-4 floor and are excluded from their curves.", "Levenberg-Marquardt discards the two largest residuals as outliers on each pass.", "Two rows share a duplicate saturation and the table validator keeps only the first of the pair."]'::jsonb, 1, 'krw at Swc and kro at 1 - Sor are hard zeros, one on each curve, so each curve contributes 12 usable points. The LM kernel never discards outliers, no rows are merged, and the grid has no duplicate saturations.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 4, 'On the Ekene grid the fit converges in 4 iterations to nw 2.4999999999999996 with rmsLog 1.3784958753881249e-16 and r2Log 1. What is the correct reading?', '["The optimizer overfit measurement noise and the exponents should be treated as unreliable.", "A residual of that magnitude signals a unit mismatch between the two curves.", "An r2Log of exactly 1 indicates the solver stopped at its iteration cap.", "The table was generated by the Corey model itself, so the fit recovers the plant exactly."]'::jsonb, 3, 'An rms log-residual at 1e-16 is floating-point epsilon territory: the model passes through every usable point because the points came from the model. Real lab data never fits this well. Four iterations is far under the 60 cap, and nothing about the result suggests units or noise problems.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 5, 'The 95 percent interval for nw comes back as [2.4999999999999996, 2.4999999999999996] on the synthetic grid. On real lab data, what would a WIDE interval on an exponent tell you?', '["The fit failed to converge, so the parameters should be discarded outright.", "The exponent is poorly constrained and should be quoted with its uncertainty.", "The krFloor was set too low and must be raised before the fit result can be trusted at all.", "The endpoints were left free when they should have been fixed to the table."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The interval collapses onto the point estimate only because the data is noise-free. On scattered lab points the same machinery reports how far the exponent could move without materially worsening the fit; a wide interval is a statement about the data, not a failure of the solver.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 6, 'Switching to fitEndpoints: true on the Ekene grid returns nw 2.5, krwMax 0.3, kroMax 0.9 with rmsLog 0. What changed in the optimization?', '["The parameter vector grew from [nw, no] to [nw, no, krwMax, kroMax], the kr endpoints now free.", "The exponent bounds widened beyond [0.5, 8] so that the endpoint values could be admitted as well.", "The residuals switched from log10 mismatches to plain linear differences.", "The saturation endpoints Swc and Sor themselves became fit parameters."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the endpoint kr VALUES join the parameter vector; the saturation endpoints stay fixed by the table, the exponent bounds stay [0.5, 8], and the residuals stay logarithmic. On exact data the four-parameter fit lands on the plant with zero residual.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 7, 'A contractor''s table header claims Corey exponents 2.5 and 2.0, but the rows were actually generated with nw 1.2 and no 3.5. What does fitCoreyToKrTable return?', '["nw 2.5 and no 2.0, because the header seeds the initial guess and exact data cannot move it.", "A refusal, because the header metadata and the fitted values disagree.", "nw 1.2 and no 3.5000000000000004: the fit reads the rows, not the header.", "Compromise values near nw 1.85 and no 2.75, splitting header against data."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The initial guess is always [2, 2] regardless of any header, and the optimizer walks to whatever the rows support. The engine never sees metadata claims, so it can neither honor them nor object to them. The recovered 3.5000000000000004 is the plant to machine precision.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 8, 'On the normalized axis, the Ekene mid row reads krwN 0.17677669529663698. Which computation produced it?', '["0.9 multiplied by 0.25, the oil curve''s raw value at the same saturation.", "0.05303300858899109 divided by 0.9, that is the raw water value over the oil curve''s endpoint.", "0.5 raised to 1.5, one power below the water exponent.", "0.5 raised to the water exponent 2.5: normalization leaves the exponent untouched."]'::jsonb, 3, 'krwN divides the raw krw by its own endpoint: (0.3 x 0.5^2.5) / 0.3 = 0.5^2.5. The frame cancels and the exponent survives, which is the whole point of normalizing. Dividing by the OTHER curve''s endpoint, or dropping a power, produces different numbers.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 9, 'What does endpoint normalization remove from a rel-perm table, and what does it keep?', '["It removes the frame, Swc, Sor and the endpoint kr values, and keeps the shapes.", "It removes the exponents so that endpoint frames can be compared directly across plugs.", "It removes measurement noise while preserving every raw kr value unchanged.", "It removes the water curve so that oil curves can be compared on an axis of their own."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Normalization maps every table onto Swn in [0, 1] with each curve scaled by its own endpoint. What remains is pure shape. It has no denoising power and it treats both curves symmetrically.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 10, 'The Ekene shape is rescaled onto target endpoints Swc 0.25, Sor 0.3, krwMax 0.45, kroMax 0.85. What is the LAST row of the scaled table?', '["Sw 0.75, krw 0.3, kro 0, carrying the source frame''s ceiling across.", "Sw 0.7, krw 0.45, kro 0.", "Sw 0.7, krw 0.85, kro 0, taking the oil endpoint for the water curve.", "Sw 0.75, krw 0.45, kro 0.05, ending above the residual with live oil."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The scaled table ends at Sw = 1 - Sor(target) = 0.7, where the water curve takes the target''s own krwMax 0.45 and the oil curve is definitionally zero. Carrying the source''s 0.75 or 0.3 across is exactly what rescaling exists to prevent.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 11, 'A shape transfer via scaleKrTable is trustworthy only when source and target share what?', '["The same porosity and permeability, since those set the curve shapes.", "The same fluid viscosities, since kr curves are always measured in specific fluid pairs.", "The same pore-scale physics: rock family, wettability and displacement direction.", "The same number of table rows, since the resampler pairs rows one to one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Transfer assumes the normalized shape is a property of the rock-fluid system''s physics. Porosity and permeability live in the frame, not the shape; viscosities enter fractional flow, not kr; and the resampler generates its own rows, so row counts are irrelevant.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 12, 'The normalized oil value at the midpoint is kroN 0.2499999999999999. Which exponent does that reveal?', '["no = 2: the value is (1 - 0.5) squared.", "no = 2.5, matching the water curve''s exponent on the shared axis.", "no = 4, since one quarter is the fourth part of unity.", "no = 1, a straight line between the oil curve''s endpoints."]'::jsonb, 0, 'kroN at Swn 0.5 is (1 - 0.5)^no. Getting 0.25 pins no = 2. An exponent of 2.5 gives 0.177, an exponent of 4 gives 0.0625, and a straight line gives 0.5; each is a different curve.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 13, 'Two plugs from the same sand overlay almost perfectly on the Swn axis but sit far apart in raw kr. What is the correct conclusion?', '["One plug''s measurements must be wrong, since plugs from a single sand should overlay in raw kr.", "The overlay is coincidence, because normalized comparisons carry no physical meaning.", "The plugs differ in wettability and the normalization has concealed it.", "They share exponents and differ only in their endpoint frames, which normalization exposes."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Endpoint frames legitimately vary between plugs of one sand; shape is the sturdier family property. A wettability difference would bend the normalized shapes apart, not overlay them, and raw-kr disagreement between plugs is expected, not evidence of error.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 14, 'Why does the engine offer a gas-oil Corey set but no three-phase relative permeability model?', '["Three-phase relative permeability cannot be expressed in Corey form at all.", "The owner''s thin-real lock: two two-phase systems are provided, three-phase is out of scope.", "The gas-oil set already covers three-phase behaviour once water sits at connate.", "The numerical precision available inside the engine cannot support three-phase relative permeability tables."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gas-oil set is defined at connate water and is still a two-phase description; treating it as three-phase coverage is the misconception. Three-phase Corey-style models do exist in the literature. The absence here is a scope decision, recorded in the engine header, not a capability accident.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 15, 'The averaged mean curve refits to a = 0.2491501585202375, while the single-plug direct fit with the same Swirr returns a = 0.25 exactly. Which value should a report quote for the field curve?', '["The averaged value, because a mean of three plugs always beats any single plug.", "The direct value, because the averaging machinery is numerically unstable.", "Either can be defended, but only together with the chain that produced it.", "Their arithmetic mean, splitting the difference between the two chains."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both numbers are correct outputs of correct procedures; they differ because the averaging chain resamples before refitting. The reporting rule is provenance: name the chain. Averaging is not unstable, more plugs is not automatically better on synthetic agreement, and splitting differences manufactures a third, unowned number.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 16, 'What mechanism moves the averaged refit off the plant even though every plug was generated from one exact curve?', '["Each plug is resampled through a log-linear tabulated evaluator before the geometric mean, and a chord under a curve that is convex in log space sits systematically off it.", "Floating-point roundoff near 1e-16 accumulates across the grid nodes into the fitted parameters.", "The geometric mean is the wrong average for log-distributed J values.", "The resampling error is random, so it cancels across plugs instead of biasing the refit."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The drift is structural, not stochastic: log-linear interpolation between grid nodes bows consistently to one side of a convex curve, so every resampled point carries the same-signed error and the refit inherits it. Roundoff is ten orders of magnitude too small, and the geometric mean is the RIGHT mean for J.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 17, 'In the recorded drift, which way do the refit parameters move relative to the plant a 0.25, b 1.0?', '["Both parameters come back high, since interpolation overshoots a convex curve.", "a comes back high and b low, preserving the product of the two parameters.", "Both come back low by the same factor, a uniform shrinkage of the curve.", "a comes back 0.34 percent low and b comes back 1.03 percent high."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The chord sits below the curve in log space, which flattens the low-saturation end less than the high end; the refit compensates with a slightly steeper b and a slightly smaller a. The two directions are opposite, and no product is preserved.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 18, 'The averaged refit reports r2Log 0.9998442671274563. What does that near-perfect statistic actually certify?', '["That the refit parameters are unbiased estimates of the curve that generated the plugs.", "Only that the mean curve is power-law-like; both fitted parameters are biased.", "That every plug shared the same irreducible saturation during averaging.", "That the geometric mean and the arithmetic mean coincided on the grid."]'::jsonb, 1, 'r2 measures how well the fitted form tracks the fitted DATA, and the resampled mean is itself a beautifully smooth curve, just not quite the plant. A statistic computed inside a chain cannot certify the chain. This is the sharpest sentence of the module: near-perfect r2, biased parameters.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 19, 'The capstone grades avg_refit_a with tolerance 0.0005. Why does submitting the design value 0.25 fail?', '["The graded chain is the averaged refit, whose drift of about 0.00085 exceeds the tolerance; the plant value answers a different chain.", "0.25 sits outside the fitter''s parameter bounds and is rejected before grading.", "The tolerance is relative rather than absolute, and 0.25 misses it on that basis.", "The capstone field actually grades the exponent b, not the prefactor a."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field asks for what averageJCurves'' refit returns, 0.2491501585202375, and the absolute tolerance 0.0005 was chosen so the plant value fails by construction. That design forces the learner to run the graded chain rather than quote the known plant.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 20, 'The 0.48345033394940007 column average was produced by a 2000-interval trapezoid from the contact to the crest. What field-wide claim does it support?', '["That the field as a whole books 13 saturation points too dry in every cell.", "That multiplying it by the net-to-gross ratio yields the field-average water saturation.", "None directly: it is the crest column''s own average, not the field''s.", "That the NG5 volumetric booking should be replaced by this number outright."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Shorter columns are wetter and the 169 cells have 169 different heights, so the crest column is the EXTREME case, not the representative one. Net-to-gross is a thickness ratio, not a saturation operator. The honest claim stops at the one column that was integrated.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 21, 'The flat Sw 0.35 the NG5 booking used is exactly right at which location in the Ekene column?', '["At the free water level, where the saturation-height model pins the rock to water saturation.", "At the crest of the structure, the single highest point; every metre below it is wetter.", "Halfway down the transition zone, balancing wetter rock below against drier above.", "Nowhere: the booking''s flat value was an arbitrary convention with no anchor."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The capillary design drains the crest to 0.35063, so the booking value is the crest value. At the free water level Sw is 1, and the halfway point of the transition zone is far wetter than 0.35. The flat booking is anchored, but at the driest point of the column.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 22, 'For the designed Ekene dip case (k 250 md, A 20000 ft2, qt 2000 rb/d, dip 10 degrees updip, gammaW 1.03, gammaO 0.8654434250764526), what gravity coefficient does makeFwFunction report?', '["0.0774684339580311", "0.004841777122376944", "-0.019367108489507776", "0.019367108489507776"]'::jsonb, 3, 'At the designed rate the coefficient is 0.019367108489507776. The larger value belongs to the 500 rb/d case, the smaller to 8000 rb/d, and the negated value is the downdip case at the design rate; the coefficient scales as one over qt and flips sign with the dip.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 23, 'With the dip term on, the updip breakthrough displacement efficiency for the designed case is:', '["0.5095807170488317", "0.5088773453049006", "0.5081700834294871", "0.5771964898801638"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Updip gravity lifts EDbt to 0.5095807170488317. The middle value 0.5088773453049006 is the flat case, 0.5081700834294871 is the downdip case, and 0.5771964898801638 belongs to the polymer screening case, a different lever entirely.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 24, 'Cutting total rate from 2000 to 1000 rb/d does what to the gravity coefficient?', '["Halves it to 0.009683554244753888, since slower floods feel less gravity.", "Leaves it unchanged: rate enters only the Welge construction, never the gravity coefficient.", "Doubles it to 0.03873421697901555, since the coefficient scales as one over qt.", "Quadruples it to 0.0774684339580311, since the scaling runs with qt squared."]'::jsonb, 2, 'qt sits in the denominator, so halving the rate doubles the coefficient: gravity gets more time per unit of throughput. The quadrupled value is real but belongs to the 500 rb/d case, a factor of four in rate, not two.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 25, 'At 8000 rb/d the front saturation reads 0.6372, identical to the flat case. What is the right reading?', '["Gravity switches off entirely once the rate passes a threshold.", "The dip shift fell below the scan''s 0.0004 grid step: real, but under its resolution.", "The scan failed to converge at high rate and returned its default front saturation instead.", "Updip and downdip displacements coincide exactly at 8000 rb/d."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The coefficient at 8000 rb/d is 0.004841777122376944, small but not zero, and EDbt still nudges to 0.5090537496023166. The FRONT lands on the same grid point because the scan resolves saturations only to 0.0004. Settings, not physics: a finer scan would separate them.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 26, 'The engine''s sign convention for the dip term is:', '["Positive alpha means the reservoir dips away from the injector, regardless of flow direction.", "The sign follows the injector''s structural position rather than the direction of flow.", "The sign of alpha has no effect, because the dip enters through a squared sine.", "Positive alpha means the displacement moves updip, which reduces fw and helps the flood; a negative alpha models downdip displacement, where gravity works against it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The header states it plainly: displacement updip is positive. Denser water pushed up-structure is held back by gravity, trimming fw. The term carries sin(alpha) to the first power, so the sign survives and flips the effect for downdip floods.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 27, 'Rank the three breakthrough efficiencies for the designed case: downdip, flat, updip.', '["Downdip 0.5081700834294871, then flat 0.5088773453049006, then updip 0.5095807170488317, separated by about 0.0007 each side.", "Flat lowest, then downdip, then updip, since any dip term adds energy to the flood.", "Updip lowest, then flat, then downdip, because water runs downhill fastest.", "All three equal within machine precision at the designed field rate."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The flat case sits exactly between the two dipped cases: updip gravity holds water back and lifts efficiency, downdip does the opposite. The 0.0007 separations are why the capstone''s 0.0005 tolerances can tell all three apart.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 28, 'When does analyzeDisplacement attach its gravity-stable warning?', '["Whenever the dip angle entered exceeds 10 degrees, which the engine treats as a range check.", "When the gravity coefficient comes out negative, flagging a downdip flood.", "When the gravity assist is strong enough to clamp fw at zero over part of the mobile range.", "When the Welge tangent construction fails to find a front saturation."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exact string is ''Gravity assist is strong enough to hold fw at zero over part of the saturation range (gravity-stable displacement).'' It fires on the clamped-fw condition, not on any angle threshold or sign; downdip floods run without warnings, and the tangent still exists.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 29, 'polymerMuMult 4 on the Ekene case yields which effective water viscosity?', '["4 cp, the multiplier standing in for the viscosity itself.", "2 cp: the multiplier acts on the 0.5 cp base water.", "0.125 cp, the base viscosity divided by the multiplier.", "1.8 cp, thickened water matching the oil exactly."]'::jsonb, 1, 'muWeff = 0.5 x 4 = 2 cp. The multiplier is a factor, not a viscosity; dividing runs the lever backwards; and matching the oil at 1.8 cp is close to but not what this multiplier produces.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 30, 'The polymer case''s endpoint mobility ratio is:', '["1.2", "4.8", "0.6", "0.3"]'::jsonb, 3, 'M = (0.3/2)/(0.9/1.8) = 0.3: thickened water is now the less mobile phase. 1.2 is the base case, 4.8 multiplies the base M by the polymer factor in the wrong direction, and 0.6 halves it instead of quartering it.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 31, 'With polymer at multiplier 4, the Welge front saturation moves from 0.6372 to:', '["0.7044", "0.6376", "0.75", "0.6807702744481854"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The flatter mobility contrast lets the tangent touch far higher on the fw curve: Swf 0.7044. The value 0.6376 is the updip gravity case, 0.75 is the mobile ceiling 1 - Sor, and 0.6807702744481854 is the BASE case''s average saturation behind the front, a different quantity altogether.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 32, 'Breakthrough displacement efficiency under polymer is:', '["0.5095807170488317, the same gain the dip term delivers.", "0.6153846153846154, the ultimate ceiling reached already at breakthrough.", "0.5771964898801638, about 0.068 above the base 0.5088773453049006.", "0.33077027444818546, unchanged because polymer only delays water."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Polymer buys roughly 6.8 saturation points of ED at breakthrough, an order of magnitude more than the dip term''s 0.0007. The ceiling is not reached at breakthrough even with polymer, and 0.33077027444818546 is the base QiBt, pore volumes rather than an efficiency.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 33, 'Why does EDmax stay at 0.6153846153846154 no matter the polymer multiplier?', '["The engine caps displacement efficiency at that value numerically.", "The ceiling is set by the endpoints alone, (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc); viscosity changes the path to the ceiling, never the ceiling.", "Polymer raises the residual oil exactly as much as it raises the front.", "It does not stay: with polymer the ceiling rises toward 0.75."]'::jsonb, 1, 'EDmax is closed-form in Swc and Sor, which no injection-side lever touches. Nothing is capped numerically, polymer leaves Sor alone in this screening model, and 0.75 is the TEXTBOOK sample case''s ceiling, not Ekene''s.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 34, 'The engine attaches this to every polymer run: ''Polymer case: water viscosity multiplied for screening only; no adsorption, permeability reduction, or rheology effects.'' What is the right operational reading?', '["Treat the results as a screening bound: the real flood adds adsorption, permeability and rheology effects the model deliberately omits.", "The warning is a deprecation notice: the polymer input is being retired.", "The warning means the run failed and silently returned base-case values.", "It flags that the multiplier exceeded the allowed screening range."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The run is valid and the numbers are exact for the model as stated; the warning defines the model''s edge. Real polymer floods lose viscosity to shear, lose polymer to adsorption, and alter the rock, none of which is in this screen. Nothing failed and nothing is deprecated.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 35, 'Under polymer the pore volumes injected at breakthrough RISE from 0.33077027444818546 to 0.3751777184221064, yet the flood is better. Why is later breakthrough good here?', '["It is not actually better: later breakthrough always wastes injection water.", "The extra pore volumes cost nothing once polymer is in the water.", "Because the capstone grades breakthrough timing, later is defined as better.", "The tangent touches higher on the fw curve, so the average saturation behind the front, and with it ED, is higher when water finally arrives."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Later breakthrough in PV terms means the front itself carries more displaced oil ahead of it: SwAvgBt rises with Swf. The judgment is physical, not definitional, and injected volume is never free; the point is what each injected volume displaces.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 36, 'Which capstone field is produced by fitCoreyToKrTable rather than by any displacement construction?', '["gravity_ed_bt", "polymer_ed_bt", "fitted_nw", "downdip_ed_bt"]'::jsonb, 2, 'fitted_nw comes from the Levenberg-Marquardt fit to the 13-row lab grid. The three ed_bt fields all come from Welge constructions over modified fractional-flow curves: gravity updip, gravity downdip, and thickened water.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 37, 'A candidate submits the flat 0.5088773453049006 for BOTH gravity_ed_bt and downdip_ed_bt. What happens at grading?', '["Both pass, since 0.0007 rounds to zero at the stated precision.", "Both fail: 0.0007 away on each side, against a tolerance of 0.0005.", "gravity_ed_bt passes but downdip_ed_bt fails, since only the downdip side is gated.", "The submission is rejected before grading because it contains two duplicate values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tolerances were designed so the flat value fails on both sides: updip is 0.5095807170488317 and downdip is 0.5081700834294871, each about 0.0007 away. Grading is per-field against expected and tol; duplicates are not screened and rounding plays no role.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 38, 'Why does the capstone pin the column average to a NAMED rule, the composite trapezoid with 2000 intervals from contact to crest?', '["A numerical average depends on its quadrature; naming the rule makes the graded number reproducible instead of implementation-dependent.", "The trapezoid rule is exact for this integrand, so the answer is rule-free.", "The saturation profile is linear in height, so any rule would give the same answer.", "The engine refuses to integrate a profile without an explicit interval count."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sw(h) is convex, so different rules and resolutions give slightly different averages; the graded 0.48345033394940007 is the trapezoid-2000 answer specifically. Pinning the rule is the same provenance discipline as naming a fitting chain. The trapezoid is not exact on a curved profile and the profile is not linear.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 39, 'Which design lever raises the ultimate displacement ceiling EDmax?', '["Polymer at a sufficiently high viscosity multiplier.", "Slowing the flood enough to harvest the full gravity assist.", "Steepening the effective dip through well placement.", "None of rate, dip or polymer: only moving the endpoints themselves, such as achieving a lower residual oil saturation, lifts the ceiling."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every injection-side lever reshapes fw and moves the PATH: front height, breakthrough timing, efficiency at breakthrough. The ceiling (1 - Sor - Swc)/(1 - Swc) contains none of those levers. Only endpoint change, which in practice means a different displacement mechanism, moves it.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 40, 'An Expert report quotes ''nw = 2.5 (confidence interval collapsed), a = 0.2491501585202375 (refit of the resampled mean), EDbt = 0.5095807170488317 (designed updip case)''. What reporting practice do all three entries share?', '["All three are rounded to a single shared precision so that a reader can compare them directly.", "All three come from a single call to analyzeDisplacement.", "Each number is tied to the procedure that produced it, so a reader can reproduce the chain.", "All three were measured directly on Ekene core plugs in the laboratory."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tier''s reporting rule is provenance: a fitted exponent names its interval behaviour, a refit prefactor names its chain, a designed-case efficiency names its case. None of the three shares a precision or an engine call, and none is a direct laboratory measurement.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 41, 'What extra information would turning the crest-column average 0.48345033394940007 into a FIELD average require?', '["Only the net-to-gross ratio 0.8 applied as a correction factor.", "Each of the 169 oil cells'' own column height, integrated cell by cell.", "The mobility ratio 1.2, to weight the average by flow capacity.", "Nothing more: a single column is a representative sample by definition."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Column height drives the average, and heights vary across the accumulation, so each cell needs its own integral. Net-to-gross rescales thickness, not saturation; mobility ratio belongs to displacement, not to the static saturation field; and the crest column is the least representative column there is.'),
  ('scal', 'advanced', 'final', null, 42, 'Which of these engine outcomes is a WARNING attached to a completed run rather than a refusal?', '["The gravity-stable notice, when fw is clamped at zero over part of the saturation range.", "A capillary pressure table whose values increase as water saturation increases along the curve.", "A rel-perm table with krw positive at its lowest saturation.", "Corey parameters whose Swc and Sor leave no mobile saturation range."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gravity-stable and polymer-screening messages ride along with valid results in the warnings array. The other three are validation failures: a rising Pc drainage table, mobile connate water, and an empty mobile window each stop the calculation with an error instead.');
